Another idea: an indication of how many background jobs are currently running. With many terminal tabs open, I can forget if I already have $EDITOR (or something else) running, so the number of jobs can be a nice cue.<p><pre><code>    _jobscount() {
        local jobs=($(jobs -p))
        local count=${#jobs[@]}
        (($count)) &amp;&amp; echo -n &quot; (${count}j)&quot;
    }
</code></pre>
And then put that in your prompt.
